"Independence numbers in certain families of highly symmetric graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.16739)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Title: Independence numbers in certain families of highly symmetric graphs ... While it was shown in the originating work how various counting invariants in these families behave very regularly, not much has thus far been proven about the behaviors of the typical extremal graph theoretic invariants such as their independence and clique numbers. ... All of this work falls into a growing trend in representation stability theory that displays the regular behaviors of a number of extremal invariants that arise when one looks at FI-algebras and modules.\nTitle: Independence numbers in certain families of highly symmetric graphs\nAuthors: David Guan, Eric Ramos\nDate: Tue Jan 30 04:38:28 2024\n\nFI-graphs were introduced by the second author and White to capture the idea of a family of nested graphs, each member of which is acted on by a progressively larger symmetric group. That work was built on the newly minted foundations of representation stability theory and FI-modules. Examples of such families include the complete graphs and the Kneser and Johnson graphs, among many others. While it was shown in the originating work how various counting invariants in these families behave very regularly, not much has thus far been proven about the behaviors of the typical extremal graph theoretic invariants such as their independence and clique numbers. In this paper we provide a conjecture on the growth of the independence and clique numbers in these families, and prove this conjecture in one case. We also provide computer code that generates experimental evidence in many other cases. All of this work falls into a growing trend in representation stability theory that displays the regular behaviors of a number of extremal invariants that arise when one looks at FI-algebras and modules.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExtension of the Gyárfás-Sumner conjecture to signed graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.03335)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Title: Extension of the Gyárfás-Sumner conjecture to signed graphs ... Studying structural conditions which imply bounds on the balanced chromatic number of signed graphs is among the most fundamental problems in graph theory.\nTitle: Extension of the Gyárfás-Sumner conjecture to signed graphs\nAuthors: Guillaume Aubian, Allen Ibiapina, Luis Kuffner, Reza Naserasr, Cyril Pujol, Cléophée Robin, Huan Zhou\nDate: Wed Nov  5 10:08:15 2025\n\nThe balanced chromatic number of a signed graph G is the minimum number of balanced sets that cover all vertices of G. Studying structural conditions which imply bounds on the balanced chromatic number of signed graphs is among the most fundamental problems in graph theory. In this work, we initiate the study of coloring hereditary classes of signed graphs. More precisely, we say that a set F = {F_1, F_2, ..., F_l} is a GS (for Gy\\'arf\\'as-Sumner) set if there exists a constant c such that signed graphs with no induced subgraph switching equivalent to a member of F admit a balanced c-coloring. The focus of this work is to study GS sets of order 2. We show that if F is a GS set of order 2, then F_1 is either (K_3, -) or (K_4, -), and F_2 is a linear forest. In the case of F_1 = (K_3, -), we show that any choice of a linear forest for F_2 works. In the case of F_1 = (K_4, -), we show that if each connected component of F_2 is a path of length at most 4, then {F_1, F_2} is a GS set.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIndependence numbers in certain families of highly symmetric graphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2401.16739v1)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; ## Independence numbers in certain families of highly symmetric graphs ... clique number complete graph graph group independence number kneser symmetric group representation ... FI-graphs were introduced by the second author and White to capture the idea of a family of nested graphs, each member of which is acted on by a progressively larger symmetric group.\n\n## Independence numbers in certain families of highly symmetric graphs\n\nDavid Guan, Eric Ramos\n\nPublished: 2024-01-30\n\nComments: Associated code can be found here: https://ericgramos.github.io/code.html\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\nclique number complete graph graph group independence number kneser symmetric group representation\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nFI-graphs were introduced by the second author and White to capture the idea of a family of nested graphs, each member of which is acted on by a progressively larger symmetric group. That work was built on the newly minted foundations of representation stability theory and FI-modules. Examples of such families include the complete graphs and the Kneser and Johnson graphs, among many others. While it was shown in the originating work how various counting invariants in these families behave very regularly, not much has thus far been proven about the behaviors of the typical extremal graph theoretic invariants such as their independence and clique numbers. In this paper we provide a conjecture on the growth of the independence and clique numbers in these families, and prove this conjecture in one case. We also provide computer code that generates experimental evidence in many other cases. All of this work falls into a growing trend in representation stability theory that displays the regular behaviors of a number of extremal invariants that arise when one looks at FI-algebras and modules.\n\n### BibTeX\n\n[Button: arXiv BibTeX ] [Button: Published BibTeX ]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n--------------------------