ull dimension of M n equals\n\nto the Krull dimension of the quotient ring (R/I )G n.\n\nProof. For simplicity, denote (B⊗(A+C))n as S n for now. It suﬃces to ﬁnd an isomorphism\n\nbetween S n/Ann S n(R/I)G nand (R/I)G n. Using the deﬁnition of annihilator,\n\nAnn S n(R/I)G n={s∈S n|s·m=I G n∀m∈(R/I)G n}.\n\nRecall from the proof of Lemma 3.11 that there is a surjective homomorphism ϕ n between\n\nS n and R G n, and the action is deﬁned by r·m=ϕ n(r)m. Since (R/I)G n is a quotient of\n\nR G n, this action can be naturally extended. Using this deﬁnition, we can ﬁnd\n\nAnn S n(R/I)G n\n\n={s∈S n|s·m=I G n∀m∈(R/I)G n}\n\n={s∈S n|(ϕ n(s) + I G n)(r+I G n) = I G n∀r+I G n∈(R/I)G n}\n\n={s∈S n|(ϕ n(s)r+I G n) = I G n∀r+I G n∈(R/I)G n}\n\n={s∈S n|ϕ n(s)r∈I G n∀r∈R G n}\n\n={s∈S n|ϕ n(s)∈I G n}\n\n12\n\nwhere the last equality holds because I G n is an ideal of R G n, and R G n is a ring with 1.\n\nNow denote Ann as Ann S n(R/I)G nfor simplicity, and deﬁne a function ψ n:S n/Ann −→\n\n(R/I)G n such that\n\nψ n(s+ Ann) = ϕ n(s) + I G n.\n\nFirst check that this map is well-deﬁned: suppose s 1+ Ann = s 2+ Ann, so s 1−s 2∈Ann,\n\nso ϕ n(s 1)−ϕ n(s 2) = ϕ n(s 1−s 2)∈I G n by deﬁnition, so ψ n(s 1+ Ann) = ϕ n(s 1) + I G n=\n\nBy repeatedly running the random FI-graph generator in our Sagemath program, we observe\n\nthe following patterns for any G•that is vertex-quadratic where the second degree entirely\n\ncomes from unordered-pair orbits.\n\n1. For every n≫0, the independence number α(G n)agrees with an (at most quadratic-\n\ndegree) quasi-polynomial of at most two pieces; in other words,\n\nα(G n) = f 1(n), n is odd\n\nf 2(n), n is even\n\nwhere f 1,f 2 are polynomials of degree at most two. This observation shows that\n\nconjecture 1.2 is likely to be true beyond vertex-linearity.\n\n2. If the classiﬁcation graph C corresponding to G•does not contain an orbit of the\n\nJohnson graph (meaning that a vertex v(2) with exactly a loop e(2)\n\n2 around is not an\n\ninduce