 of Graph Theory0 (2026): 1-14. https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.70047. 1 Introduction The four-color theorem has been the driving force behind most developments in graph theory. It is simple, beautiful, and can be stated in numerous ways, each of which offers a different insight and a different direction of study.\n\n#### [2511.03335] Extension of the Gyárfás-Sumner conjecture to signed graphs\n\nNov 05, 2025 |\n\nBy Allen Ibiapina, Luis Küffner, Reza Naserasr, Cyril Pujol\n\n| arXiv\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nadditive-combinatorics – Random Permutations (https://seaneberhard.com/category/additive-combinatorics/)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Such a conjecture was recently posed by Wood and stated in a paper of Devillers, Kamcev, McKay, O Cathain, Royle, Van de Voorde, Wanless, and Wood.\n\n> Conjecture 1 For every integer ${t \\ge 2}$ there are only finitely many triangle-free ${K_{2,t}}$-free graph with diameter ${2}$ apart from stars.\nSuch a conjecture was recently posed by Wood and stated in a paper of Devillers, Kamcev, McKay, O Cathain, Royle, Van de Voorde, Wanless, and Wood.\n\n> Conjecture 1 For every integer ${t \\ge 2}$ there are only finitely many triangle-free ${K_{2,t}}$-free graph with diameter ${2}$ apart from stars.\n\nFor ${t = 2}$ this is immediate from Hoffman–Singleton. The authors (Devillers, Kamcev, …) find almost ${6}$ million examples of such graphs with ${t=3}$, but “nothing suggestive of an infinite class”. However, the following construction (which arose in conversations with Padraig O Cathain) disproves the conjecture for ${t = 7}$.\n\nLet ${p}$ be a prime congruent to ${11}$ mod ${12}$. This condition ensures that ${-1}$ and ${-3}$ are quadratic nonresidues. Let ${G = \\mathbf{F}_p^2}$ and let ${A = \\{(x, \\pm x^2) : x \\in \\mathbf{F}_p, x \\ne 0\\}}$. Now consider the Cayley graph ${\\mathrm{Cay}(G, A)}$. Equivalently, this is the graph with vertex set $\\mathbf{Z}/p\\mathbf{Z} \\times \\mathbf{Z}/p\\mathbf{Z}$ and $(x_1, y_1)$ joined to $(x_2, y_2)$ if and only if $y_1 - y_2 = \\pm (x_1 - x_2)^2 \\ne 0$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Luis Kuffner (https://dblp.org/pid/421/7809.html)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Extension of the Gyárfás-Sumner conjecture to signed graphs. ... last updated on 2026-06-27 06:48 CEST by the dblp team\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2511-03335\n\nGuillaume Aubian, Allen Ibiapina, Luis Kuffner, Reza Naserasr, Cyril Pujol, Cléophée Robin, Huan Zhou:\nExtension of the Gyárfás-Sumner conjecture to signed graphs. CoRR abs/2511.03335 (2025)\n\n## Coauthor Index\n\nsee FAQ\n\n  * What is the meaning of the colors in the coauthor index?\n  * How does dblp detect coauthor communities?\n\n1\n\nGuillaume Aubian\n\n[i2] [i1]\n\n2\n\nXiaolan Hu\n\n[j1]\n\n3\n\nAllen Ibiapina\n\n[i1]\n\n4\n\nJiaao Li\n\n[j1]\n\n5\n\nReza Naserasr\n\n[j1] [i1]\n\n6\n\nCyril Pujol\n\n[i1]\n\n7\n\nCléophée Robin\n\n[i1]\n\n8\n\nLujia Wang\n\n[j1]\n\n9\n\nZhouningxin Wang\n\n[j1]\n\n10\n\nXiaowei Yu\n\n[j1]\n\n11\n\nHuan Zhou\n\n[i1]\n\nlast updated on 2026-06-27 06:48 CEST by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublications | Eric Ramos (https://ericgramos.github.io/papers.html)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * Computing stable homology representations of graph configuration spaces ...   * Independence numbers in certain families of highly symmetric graphs ...   * The weak categorical quiver minor theorem and its applications: matchings, mul